WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.205
The launch chart says a small adapter takes Qwen's twenty-seven billion parameter

00:00:05.205 --> 00:00:07.583
model from twenty-nine percent to sixty-three.

00:00:07.963 --> 00:00:09.784
The model card now says thirty-nine.

00:00:09.784 --> 00:00:13.731
Nine of the base model's runs had died on a broken network tunnel.

00:00:15.748 --> 00:00:16.719
Hi, I'm Banjo.

00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:19.633
I read the adapter's tensors and three model cards.

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:23.651
Same task under pi.

00:00:23.651 --> 00:00:27.503
The adapter greps, reads, edits, tests, and is done in eight calls.

00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:31.121
The base model keeps probing with inline Python until it runs out

00:00:31.121 --> 00:00:32.200
of its one hundred.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.270
I read the adapter's header by range request.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.273
All sixty-four layers get an adapter on the MLP.

00:00:39.693 --> 00:00:41.626
Only sixteen get attention adapters.

00:00:41.626 --> 00:00:46.650
The other forty-eight are linear attention, whose names the target list never matches.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.918
Training runs pi itself, in a sandbox, on bug fixes and terminal tasks.

00:00:53.318 --> 00:00:56.367
vLLM keeps the exact tokens the policy sampled.

00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:58.786
The task's tests decide right or wrong.

00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:01.183
Eight rollouts of each task are compared.

00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:03.777
And the new adapter is loaded by name.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.259
The reward is one for passing the tests, plus at most a

00:01:09.259 --> 00:01:10.914
tenth for using fewer tool calls.

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:12.642
GRPO compares the group.

00:01:12.642 --> 00:01:15.583
When some fail, being right is the whole signal.

00:01:16.003 --> 00:01:19.209
Once all eight pass, they differ only in how many calls they took.

00:01:20.203 --> 00:01:23.298
Normalised, that tiny bonus becomes the entire signal.

00:01:23.298 --> 00:01:25.619
Short solutions are pushed up hard.

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:30.904
Tool calls per solved training rollout fall steadily in every run.

00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:36.203
Under pi, the hard half of SWE-bench Pro goes from thirty-nine

00:01:36.203 --> 00:01:37.599
to about sixty-five percent.

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:42.394
Terminal-Bench goes from about forty to fifty-six, and holds on held-out tasks.

00:01:43.883 --> 00:01:47.947
Thinking at full effort still solves a little more on SWE-bench Pro,

00:01:47.947 --> 00:01:50.656
not significantly, at about five times the tokens.

00:01:52.317 --> 00:01:55.995
On a 3090, in a harness with other tool names, it never

00:01:55.995 --> 00:01:59.059
called a pi tool and solved seventeen of eighteen runs.

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:03.197
Under pi, on easy tasks, it missed three the base solved, each

00:02:03.197 --> 00:02:05.365
time stopping after three or four calls.

00:02:06.683 --> 00:02:09.970
A harness adapter makes the model decisive in one agent.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.927
Whether it is more accurate depends on the agent.

00:02:14.525 --> 00:02:17.876
A small LoRA, a reward for passing and then for brevity.

00:02:17.876 --> 00:02:18.790
Thanks for watching!

00:02:19.209 --> 00:02:21.228
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:21.228 --> 00:02:21.805
I'm Banjo.

00:02:21.805 --> 00:02:22.093
Bye!
